   "reasoning_summary": "The formula C6H13NO6 has DoU 1, which is accounted for by the single carbonyl at 175.6 (6), typical of an amide or acid. The other five carbons (75.4, 73.4, 70.0, 65.5, 61.1) are all oxygenated sp3 carbons, consistent with a C6 sugar-acid chain bearing five OH groups. With the N placed as CONH2, the structure is gluconamide (or one of its stereoisomers). In D2O the 7 exchangeable protons (5 OH and NH2) are not observed. The 6 C\u2013H protons appear at 4.42 (H2, deshielded by the adjacent C=O) and 3.66\u20133.88. The amino-acid isomer would need a C\u2013N carbon near 56 ppm, which is absent, so it ranks lower. Stereochemistry cannot be assigned from these data."
